  "slug": "single-nucleotide-precision-67f0",
  "summary": "Recent preprints extend somatic mutation mapping to normal tissues, cell-free DNA, and InDel signatures, sharpening mutational process inference.",
  "synthesis": "Across these preprints, the dominant thread is the resolution at which somatic mutational processes are attributed, ranging from allele-level and sequence-context effects to chromosome-level and indel-level variation. A heterozygous MUTYH carrier shows the oxidative signature SBS18 across multiple normal human tissues, and the DNA language model EAGLE-MUT predicts context-dependent somatic mutation probabilities that vary with sequence context and nucleosome positioning. Chromosome-level instability appears in acral melanoma, where single-cell chromosome counts range from 25 to over 200 and are linked to MMBIR repair. Two cell-free DNA studies extend detection of clonal mosaicism and liver-derived somatic mutations to chronic liver disease and ulcerative colitis without tissue sampling. A genome-wide InDel taxonomy of 887 multiple myeloma genomes adds a distinct class of mutational processes, including a TXNDC5 hotspot and an association with treatment outcome.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
